            <ArticleTitle>Femoral artery stenosis following percutaneous closure using a starclose closure device.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>814-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00270-008-9341-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Starclose (Abbott Vascular Devices, Redwood City, CA) is a new arterial closure device that seals a femoral puncture site with an extravascular star-shaped nitinol clip. The clip projects small tines into the arterial wall which fold inward, causing the arterial wall to pucker, producing a purse-string-like seal closing the puncture site. The case history is that of a 76-year-old female patient who underwent day-case percutaneous diagnostic coronary angiography. A Starclose femoral artery closure device was used to achieve hemostasis with subsequent femoral artery stenosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
